==================================================
Lab 14: Blind SQL Injection with Time Delays
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab introduces a third way of detecting whether our SQL injection is working, completely different from
anything used before. In lab 11, we watched for a specific piece of text appearing or disappearing. In lab 12
and 13, we watched for errors or error messages. Here, there is no visible text difference, and no error at all.
Instead, we measure how long the server takes to respond. If we can make the database deliberately pause for a
noticeable amount of time, and we can control exactly when that pause happens, we have found a new, purely
timing-based way to confirm our injection works.

Goal: inject a payload that causes the database to pause for a fixed number of seconds, and confirm this by
measuring how long the response actually takes.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

Just like labs 11, 12, and 13, the vulnerable input here is the TrackingId cookie. Its value is placed directly
into a SQL query without being checked or cleaned, which lets us inject our own SQL through it.

=============================
WHAT IS A TIME-BASED ATTACK

A time-based attack relies on a simple idea: if we can make the database execute something that takes a
specific, predictable amount of time, such as pausing for exactly ten seconds, we can confirm this happened
purely by measuring elapsed time on our end, without needing to look at the response content at all. This
becomes especially useful in situations where the application gives absolutely no other outward sign of whether
our injected condition was true or false.

=============================
THE FUNCTION USED HERE: PG_SLEEP

PostgreSQL databases include a built-in function that does exactly one thing, pause query execution for a given
number of seconds, then continue as normal. By calling this function with the number ten, we are asking the
database to deliberately wait ten full seconds before returning any results at all.

Since our injected SQL becomes part of the overall query that runs before the page can finish loading, forcing
this pause means the entire page takes however many extra seconds we specified to load, which we can observe
directly as a real, measurable delay on our side.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

The cookie value sent is: the original tracking ID value, followed by a single quote, then two vertical bar
symbols, then pg_sleep with the number ten inside parentheses, then two dashes at the end.

The single quote closes the original string value early, the double pipes concatenate our sleep function’s
result onto the end of that string, matching the same concatenation syntax used earlier in lab 6 for joining
username and password together. The two dashes then comment out anything that would normally follow, keeping
the rest of the query syntactically valid.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept a request containing the TrackingId cookie using Burp Suite.
Modify the cookie value by appending a single quote, two vertical bars, pg_sleep with ten inside parentheses,
and two dashes at the end.
Submit the request and observe that the application visibly takes about ten extra seconds to respond, confirming
the injection successfully controls execution timing.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library for sending web requests, and the time library, which lets us measure exactly
how long something takes to run in Python.

It asks for the base address of the lab.

A session is created, and a request is sent to the home page purely to obtain a genuine TrackingId cookie from
the server, the same approach used in the earlier blind injection labs. This original value is saved, since the
injected payload needs to be built on top of it to keep the rest of the query intact.

The full payload is built using the original tracking ID, a single quote, two vertical bars, pg_sleep with ten
inside parentheses, and two dashes at the end, exactly matching the manual steps.

Before sending the request, the current time is recorded using time.time(), which returns the current moment as
a number. The request is then sent using the same session, with the cookie set to our payload. Immediately after
the response comes back, the current time is recorded again, and the difference between these two recorded times
gives us the total elapsed time the request actually took, measured in seconds.

This elapsed time is printed, rounded to two decimal places for readability.

Finally, the script checks if the elapsed time was at least nine seconds, leaving a small buffer below the full
ten seconds to account for minor natural variation in network speed or server response time. If the delay meets
this threshold, it prints a success message confirming the injection worked. If not, it prints a failure message
suggesting the cookie name or payload syntax might need checking.

=============================
WHY WE MEASURE TIME INSTEAD OF CHECKING THE RESPONSE TEXT

Unlike every previous lab, this one gives no usable clue inside the response content itself, no special message
appears, and no error is shown, regardless of whether our condition is true or false. The only observable
difference happens in how long the entire request takes to complete, which is why we specifically track time
before and after sending the request, rather than inspecting anything about what the response actually contains.

=============================
WHY A SMALL BUFFER IS USED INSTEAD OF CHECKING FOR EXACTLY TEN SECONDS

Network requests are never perfectly precise, there is always some small amount of natural delay added by the
network itself, the server’s normal processing time, and other minor factors unrelated to our injected sleep
function. Checking for a delay of at least nine seconds, rather than requiring the delay to be exactly ten
seconds, accounts for this natural variation while still being clearly long enough to distinguish from a normal,
fast response that takes a fraction of a second.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw remains the same root cause as every earlier blind injection lab, user input, specifically
a cookie value here, reaches a SQL query without being checked or cleaned. The particular function being abused,
pg_sleep, exists as a completely normal, legitimate part of PostgreSQL, meant for things like testing or
intentionally delaying processes, but it becomes a powerful attack tool once an attacker can control when and
whether it gets called.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A time-based attack works by deliberately causing a measurable delay in the database’s response, then confirming
success purely through elapsed time, without needing any visible change in the page’s content.
PostgreSQL’s pg_sleep function is a simple, direct way to cause this kind of controlled delay, though other
databases provide their own equivalent sleep or delay functions under different names.
This technique becomes especially valuable when an application gives absolutely no other observable difference
between a true and false condition, since timing becomes the only remaining signal available to an attacker.
This lab lays the foundation for a more advanced version, where instead of always sleeping for a fixed amount of
time, the sleep is made conditional on a true or false guess, turning this timing trick into a full data
extraction technique, the same way conditional responses and conditional errors were used in earlier labs.
